As compared with the sensing effect of the \(\mathrm{Eu}_{0.47}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.53}\) -CTPCOOH PVA film for metal ions, the characteristic emission intensity of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) or \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) ion showed nonspecific enhancement or quenching to VOCs. However, these VOCs could still be easily distinguished based on characteristic emission intensity ratios of \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) ion \((I_{547}/I_{491})\) and \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ion \((I_{616}/I_{592})\) . These results clearly indicate that the \(\mathrm{Eu}_{0.47}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.53}\) -CTP-COOH PVA film is an excellent sensor for probing different VOCs. This kind of luminescent sensor for detecting different VOCs is remarkable, because it does not need any additional calibration. The interesting VOC-dependent emission of the \(\mathrm{Eu}_{0.47}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.53}\) -CTPCOOH PVA film can be ascribed to guest-dependent energy transfer from ligand CTP-COOH to \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) ions. Therefore, the characteristic emission intensity of the \(\mathrm{Eu}_{0.47}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.53}\) -CTPCOOH PVA film is relatively sensitive to the included VOCs by tuning the energy transfer from CTP-COOH to \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) ions. The emission intensity ratios of \(I_{547}/I_{491}\) for \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) ion and \(I_{616}/\) \(I_{592}\) for \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ion is almost constant and unique for each VOC guest in the luminescent spectrum of the \(\mathrm{Eu}_{0.47}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.53}\) -CTPCOOH PVA film. Such characteristic relationship between VOCs and the film sensor could be used to draw an emission-fingerprint map of VOCs based on the ratio of \(I_{616}/I_{592}\) as \(x\) -axis and the ratio of \(I_{547}/I_{491}\) as \(y\) -axis (Fig. 5c). This internal reference strategy is an excellent approach to solve the problem of variable emission intensity encountered when detecting VOCs by a single emissive transition. As shown in Fig. 5c, even for those VOCs having very similar structural motifs such as isomers of \(o\) -xylene, \(m\) -xylene, and \(\boldsymbol{p}\) -xylene, as well as homologs of benzene, toluene, and ethylbenzene, they could still be unambiguously distinguishable.
Styrene sensing in vapor. As a potential human carcinogen, styrene vapor may be harmful to skin, eyes, and respiratory system. At high levels \((10,000\,\mathrm{ppm})\) of styrene vapor, rats and pigs become comatose after several minutes, even died after 30 to \(60\,\mathrm{min}\) of exposure58. Therefore, it is necessary to develop convenient and reliable sensors for the styrene detection. However, fluorescent sensors for styrene have not been fully developed until now.
main driving force for PET is the energy gap between the lowest unoccupied molecular orbitals (LUMO) of donor and acceptor, and the donor should have higher energy level of LUMO than that of acceptor if PET is the luminescence quenching mechanism.59, 61, 62 However, as calculated by the density functional theory (DFT) at the \(\mathrm{B}3\mathrm{LYP}/6{-}31\,\mathrm{G}^{\ast}\) level (Fig. 7a), the LUMO energy level of the ligand CTP-COOH (donor) is obviously lower than that of styrene (acceptor). This result eliminates the possibility of the PET process on the luminescence quenching of the \(\mathrm{Eu}_{0.47}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.53}\) -CTP-COOH PVA film by styrene. In addition, the absorption spectrum of styrene has no obvious overlap with the emission spectrum of ligand CTP-COOH, and thus the FRET mechanism could also be ruled out. On the other hand, mild quenching effect of nitrobenzene for the fluorescence of the \(\mathrm{Eu}_{0.47}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.53}\) -CTP-COOH PVA film could be ascribed to the PET mechanism.
## Discussion
In summary, we have developed a new strategy using bimetallic \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) MOFs as self-calibrating luminescent sensors for distinguishing different metal ions in aqueous solution and VOCs in vapor. The bimetallic Ln-MOFs serve as a luminescent platform owing to excellent fingerprint correlation between metal ions/VOCs and characteristic emission intensity ratios of \(I_{547}/I_{491}\) for \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) ion and \(I_{616}/I_{592}\) for \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ion. We have successfully demonstrated the first decoded map that can effectively and reliably distinguish different metal ions in aqueous solution based on the emission intensity ratios of \(I_{616}/I_{592}\) for \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ion as the \(x\cdot\) - axis and \(I_{547}I_{491}\) for \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) ion as the \(y\) -axis. According to this decoded map, different metal ions in aqueous solution could be easily recognized. Based on a similar principle, a decoded map for sensing different VOCs in vapor has also been established. Such versatile bimetallic Ln-MOF sensor exhibits high sensitivity and selectivity for \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) ion in aqueous solution and styrene in vapor. The capability to unambiguously recognize metal ions/VOCs makes bimetallic Ln-MOFs a promising luminescent platform with potentially valuable applications.
## Methods
Materials and methods. All reagents and chemicals were purchased commercially (Sigma-Aldrich and Alfa) and used without further purifications unless otherwise specified. FT-IR spectra were carried out using a Nicolet Is-10 (Nicolet) Fourier Transform Infrared Spectrometer. TGA was made with TA Q50 at a heating rate of \(15\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\) under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere and over a temperature range from room temperature to \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.\) All the ESI-MS spectra were recorded in LCQ DECA XP mass spectrometer. NMR spectra were taken on a DRX-400 MHz (Bruker) superconducting-magnet NMR spectrometer with TMS as an internal standard. UV–Vis absorption spectrum was determined on a Shimadzu UV-3600
UV–Vis–NIR spectrophotometer. The photoluminescence measurements in the solid state and THF solution were conducted in a Shimadzu RF-5301PC fluorescence spectrophotometer. Fluorescent lifetimes were obtained with the FLS980 steady state spectrometer with a pulsed xenon lamp. ICP-MS data were obtained on an ICP-9000 \((N+M)\) inductively coupled plasma emission spectrometer. Scanning electron microscope images were performed on a JSM-6700F (JEOL) equipped with an energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) instrument. Powder XRD measurements were recorded on a Rigaku D/Max-2500 X-ray diffractometer using \(\mathrm{Cu}\ \mathrm{K}\alpha\) radiation with \(2\theta\) range of \(2{-}50^{\circ}\) , \(40\;\mathrm{KeV}\) , and \(30\,\mathrm{mA}\) having a scanning rate of \(0.01^{\circ}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) (2θ) at room temperature.
Synthesis of methyl 4-hydroxybenzoate. 4-Hydroxy benzoic acid (35 mmol, 5.3 g) and MeOH \(\left(80\,\mathrm{mL}\right)\) ) were taken in a \(150\,\mathrm{mL}\) round-bottom flask fitted with a reflux condenser. Concentrated sulfuric acid \(\left(2.5\,\mathrm{mL}\right)\) ) was added as a catalyst and the contents were refluxed for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) . The reaction mixture was cooled and excess MeOH was removed using a rotary evaporator. The residue obtained was dissolved in chloroform and washed thoroughly with \(10\%\) sodium bicarbonate solution. The organic layer was dried over anhydrous \(\mathrm{MgSO_{4}}\) and concentrated to give the product in \(95\%\) yield. \(^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR \({}^{400}\,\mathrm{Hz}\) , \(\mathrm{CDCl}_{3}\) ppm): 7.973–7.951 (d, 2 H, Ar–H), 6.887–6.865 (d, 2 H, Ar–OH), 5.8 (s, 1 H, Ar–OH), 3.896 (s, 3 H, \(\mathrm{COOCH}_{3}\) ).
Synthesis of hexa-(4-carbomethoxy-phenoxy)-cyclotriphosphazene (CTPCOOCH3). Potassium carbonate \((11.38\,\mathrm{g},\,0.082\,\mathrm{mol})\) and methyl 4-
Synthesis of hexa-(4-carbomethoxy-phenoxy)-cyclotriphosphazene (CTPCOOCH3). Potassium carbonate \((11.38\,\mathrm{g},\,0.082\,\mathrm{mol})\) and methyl 4-hydroxybenzoate \((6.54\,\mathrm{g},0.043\,\mathrm{mol})\) were added into a \(250\,\mathrm{mL}\) round-bottom flask, and then methyl 4-hydroxybenzoate was dissolved by dry THF \(\left(120\,\mathrm{mL}\right)\) . The mixture solution was heated to \(70\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) , and \(\mathrm{P}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}_{6}\) \((2.49\,\mathrm{g},0.007\,\mathrm{mol})\) was added. The mixture was stirred at this temperature overnight. The solvent was removed by evaporation under a reduced pressure, and the obtained solid was redissolved in dichloromethane \(\mathrm{(70\,mL)}\) . The organic phase was washed with deionized water and then dried over anhydrous \(\mathrm{MgSO_{4}}\) . Dichloromethane was removed by evaporation under a reduced pressure, the white powder was heated at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) by vacuum oven overnight, and the desired product was obtained (6.7 g, 7.0 g, 83.7%). \(^1\mathrm{H}\) NMR (400 MHz, DMSO- \(d_{6},\) ppm): 7.77–7.79 (d, 12 H, Ar–H), 1.73–7.06 (d, 12 H, Ar–H), 3.87 (s, 18 H, \(-\mathrm{COOCH}_{3}\) ). \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) NMR \(\left(\mathrm{DCl}_{3},\,100\,\mathrm{MHz},\,\mathrm{ppm}\right)\) :165.98, 153.62, 131.35, 127.36, 120.57, 52.30.
package. Absorption corrections were applied using the Numerical Mu from Formula method (SADABS). The structure was solved by direct methods using SHELXS-97 program of the SHELXTL package and refined by using the full-matrix least squares method on \( F^{2} \) with SHELXTL-97. Anisotropic displacement parameters were applied to all non-hydrogen atoms. Hydrogen atoms were located geometrically and were added to the structure factor calculation.
Quantum yield \((\pmb{\phi})\) measurements. The quantum yield \((\Phi)\) was determined by employing the method reported in literature64, and \(\varPhi\) of a given material could be calculated by a direct comparison with standard phosphors having known \(\varPhi\) values. The quantum yield could be calculated from the following equation:
\[
\Phi=\frac{1-R_{\mathrm{ST}}}{1-R_{\mathrm{ST}}}\times\frac{I_{\mathrm{ST}}}{I_{\mathrm{x}}}\times\Phi_{\mathrm{SST}}
\]
where \(R_{\mathrm{ST}}\) and \(R_{\mathrm{x}}\) apply for these exciting radiation reflected by the standard and the sample, respectively, and \(\Phi_{\mathrm{ST}}\) is the quantum yield of the standard phosphor. The \(I_{\mathrm{ST}}\) and \(I_{\mathrm{x}}\) terms correspond to the integrated photon flux (photon \(\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) ) for the sample and standard phosphors. Sodium salicylate was chosen as the standard and the \(\Phi_{\mathrm{ST}}\) reported is \(55\%\) at room temperature65.
Metal ion decoding and \(\mathbf{Fe}^{3+}\) ion sensing in aqueous solution. Decoding metal ions: The luminescent platform \(\mathrm{Eu}_{0.47}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.53}\) -CTP-COOH PVA film was immersed into different metal ions \((1\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{M})\) for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) , then the emission spectra of \(\mathrm{Eu}_{0.47}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.53}\) -CTP-COOH PVA film@Metal ions were recorded \((\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}\,{=}\,280\,\mathrm{nm}\) , slit: \(5\;\mathrm{nm}\) ).
Sensing for \(F e^{3+}\) ion: The \(\mathrm{Eu}_{0.47}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.53}\) -CTP-COOH PVA film was immersed in deionized water in a quartz cell \(\left(2\,\mathrm{mL}\right)\) . Then, the \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) aqueous solution was gradually added by a micro syringe, and luminescent spectra were recorded after 3 min equilibration.
VOC decoding and styrene probing in vapors. Decoding VOCs: A home-made set-up was fabricated by placing a \(\mathrm{Eu}_{0.47}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.53}\) -CTP-COOH PVA film into the cell, followed by adding one drop \((5\,\upmu\mathrm{l})\) of VOCs (styrene, nitrobenzene, \(o\) -xylene, mxylene, \(p\) -xylene, benzene, toluene, ethylenzene, chlorobenzene, 1,2-dichlorobenzene, nitromethane, 1,4-dioxane, acetonitrile, acetone, DMF, MeOH, ethanol, THF, formaldehyde, and acetaldehyde) into the cell. The cell was then capped to form a hermetic environment, and then luminescent response of \(\mathrm{Eu}_{0.47}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.53}\) -CTPCOOH PVA film towards VOCs was recorded after \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) .
Probing for styrene in vapors: The luminescent platform \(\mathrm{Eu}_{0.47}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.53}\) -CTPCOOH PVA film was fixed into the cell, followed by adding one droplet styrene (2 \(\upmu\mathrm{L}\) OR \(5\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) ) into the cell. The cell was then capped, and time-dependent luminescent spectra were recorded.
